/*  The P-x-y diagram of ethanol/water at 70 C -- the FIXED-T mirror of the
    T-x-y (2026-11-15).

    WHAT IT SHOWS.  The bubble pressure of every liquid composition at
    343.15 K (`P_bubble`) and the vapour that first appears at it
    (`y_eqT_ethanol`), from the engine's one bubble-pressure home,
    BubblePoint::pressure.  Pure water boils at 31.47 kPa, pure ethanol at
    72.22 kPa, and the mixture peaks above both near x_ethanol = 0.9
    (72.69 kPa): the MAXIMUM-pressure azeotrope that the T-x-y shows as a
    minimum-boiling one.

    THE ROUND TRIP.  At x_ethanol = 0.4 the bubble temperature at 1 atm is
    353.500724827 K (txy_point); the bubble pressure at that temperature
    (pxy_point) returns 101325 Pa, and the vapour compositions of the two
    agree.  Both properties solve sum K_i x_i = 1, one for T and one for P;
    the golden rows hold P_bubble to 1e-6 relative.

    THE PACKAGE is hxy01_ethanol_water_1atm's: NRTL with the curated pair, an
    ideal-gas vapour.  Water's vapour pressure is its record's ANTOINE law
    (31.471 kPa at 70 C), not the IAPWS-95 reference equation, which gives
    31.20 kPa (+0.9 %): the pure-water end is the record's number, not the
    reference value.  constant/parameters/Helmholtz/IAPWS95.dat is in the
    seal only because water's record names it (for the liquid density); the
    gamma-phi package does not price Psat with it.  The azeotrope, 72.69 kPa
    near x = 0.9, agrees with the measured 72.54-72.67 kPa at x 0.895-0.902
    compiled by Voutsas et al. (2011) at 343.15 K.  One liquid is assumed (no liquid-liquid probe at fixed
    T); ethanol/water is miscible, so nothing is lost here.

    NOT VALIDATED here against a measured P-x-y; the pair's own record
    carries its evidence.  Run-only + KPI golden. */
application   choupoProps;
description   "P-x-y of ethanol/water at 70 C (bubble pressure and incipient vapour along x, a maximum-pressure azeotrope near x = 0.9) and the T_bubble -> P_bubble round trip at one composition";
verbosity     2;
